1. Autoregressive Distributed Lag (ARDL) Framework
Conceptual and Econometric Justification
The Autoregressive Distributed Lag (ARDL) framework is utilized to model the persistence of economic momentum and the asynchronous transmission of macroeconomic shocks. In a quarterly context, shifts in the business cycle often exhibit delayed effects that dissipate over several periods. Unlike standard static regressions, the ARDL model acknowledges this by allowing the dependent variable (GDP) to be determined by its own historical trajectory and the lagged values of exogenous indicators. This approach is particularly effective for small-sample macroeconomic time series, as it provides consistent estimates even when variables have differing integration orders.
