Dynamic Optimization via BIC
To prevent over-parameterization and ensure the model remains parsimonious, the lag orders (p and q_j) are not fixed. Instead, they are dynamically optimized at each forecasting step using the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC). By minimizing the BIC, the model mathematically ensures that additional lags are only included if they provide a statistically significant improvement in predictive power that outweighs the cost of increased model complexity.
2. LASSO-MIDAS: Exponentially Weighted Distributed Lags (EWDL)
Conceptual and Econometric Justification
While the ARDL model is flexible, including multiple lags for a large set of indicators quickly leads to the "curse of dimensionality" and severe multicollinearity. The LASSO-MIDAS framework (utilizing Exponentially Weighted Distributed Lags) addresses this by "smoothing" the lag structure. Rather than estimating a separate coefficient for every quarterly lag, this framework assumes that the impact of an indicator follows a logical decay—where the most recent quarter carries the highest weight and older quarters carry progressively less. This collapses high-dimensional lag data into a single, weighted aggregate, preserving critical degrees of freedom.
